{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1289 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "3bd9792a0692977dec34749b550d34f6f5bdc2fd75ad7a6642e6d4894e56f70c" :edb/db-before-t 1288 :edb/db-after-t 1289 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534601 50 #inst "2026-10-08T02:07:29.245Z" 13194139534601 true] [17592186175825 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298126 13194139534601 true] [17592186175825 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298140 13194139534601 true] [17592186175827 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298133 13194139534601 true] [17592186176411 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534601 true] [17592186176411 1100 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x-1, & x<0.5 \\\\ 4-{x}^{2}, & x\\ge 0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $(0,1)$\n- $[0,2]$\n- $[1,2]$" 13194139534601 true] [17592186176411 1100 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n$f(x) = {x - 1,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0.5 \\\\ 4 - x^{2},\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 0.5$.\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n1. $(0, 1)$\n2. $[0, 2]$\n3. $[1, 2]$" 13194139534601 false] [17592186176411 1145 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- The interval $(0,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now, consider $[0,2].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0.5}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0.5}(x-1)=(0.5)-1=-0.5, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0.5}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0.5}(4-{x}^{2})=4-(0.5)^{2}=3.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=0.5$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.5.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, consider $[1,2].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0.5,$ which is not in the interval $[1,2].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[1,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139534601 true] [17592186176414 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534601 true] [17592186176414 1100 "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y = \\log_{2} x$?\n\n1. $[MATH: (0, 5]]$\n2. $[1, 5]$\n3. $[0, 5]$" 13194139534601 false] [17592186176414 1100 "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y={log}_{2}⁡x?$ \n\n- $(0,5]$\n- $[1,5]$\n- $[0,5]$" 13194139534601 true] [17592186176414 1145 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $(0,5]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[1,5]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[1,5].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,5]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on $[0,5]$ because this interval contains points where the function is not defined. In particular, it is not defined at $x=0.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139534601 true] [17592186298126 1017 #edb/ref 17592186298127 13194139534601 true] [17592186298126 1089 "q-131068" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298126 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534601 true] [17592186298126 1100 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x, & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[0,2]$\n- $[2,4]$\n- $[0,1)$" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298126 1113 #uuid "80e64203-3ebf-520c-9458-47cebefc9b47" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298126 1145 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[0,2].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(2x)=2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[2,4].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,4].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, the interval $[0,1)$, is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139534601 true] [17592186298127 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298128 13194139534601 true] [17592186298127 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298129 13194139534601 true] [17592186298127 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298130 13194139534601 true] [17592186298127 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298131 13194139534601 true] [17592186298127 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298132 13194139534601 true] [17592186298127 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534601 true] [17592186298127 1041 "selection" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298127 1104 #edb/ref 17592186298132 13194139534601 true] [17592186298127 1110 #uuid "3a6e3706-6942-5eff-9b6e-f3fe5fee27eb" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298128 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298128 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534601 true] [17592186298128 1111 #uuid "2c606cf5-2457-5244-a905-93c19f3cb6c6" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298129 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298129 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534601 true] [17592186298129 1111 #uuid "52b2d2c8-64fb-5367-aec2-909927a912d9" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298130 1034 "I only" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298130 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534601 true] [17592186298130 1111 #uuid "bb23c4b5-cb59-55e0-89d8-86263dbdf556" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298131 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298131 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534601 true] [17592186298131 1111 #uuid "cd923881-c910-5b58-8665-f91af3cf6340" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298132 1034 "II only" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298132 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534601 true] [17592186298132 1111 #uuid "0e115e35-2205-572a-a47e-d2fc46832408" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298133 1017 #edb/ref 17592186298134 13194139534601 true] [17592186298133 1089 "q-73482" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298133 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534601 true] [17592186298133 1100 "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{x+1}}?$   \n\n- $[-1,0]$\n- $[1,10]$\n- $(-1,0)$" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298133 1113 #uuid "9d2b162e-d0d6-5092-9ce3-d107896e0e87" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298133 1145 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,0]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because it is not defined at $x=-1.$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[1,10]$ is  closed, and the function is continuous on $[1,10].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval  $(-1,0)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139534601 true] [17592186298134 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298135 13194139534601 true] [17592186298134 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298136 13194139534601 true] [17592186298134 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298137 13194139534601 true] [17592186298134 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298138 13194139534601 true] [17592186298134 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298139 13194139534601 true] [17592186298134 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534601 true] [17592186298134 1041 "selection" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298134 1104 #edb/ref 17592186298139 13194139534601 true] [17592186298134 1110 #uuid "a988c3b3-2492-5d62-88fc-547620a62e48" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298135 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298135 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534601 true] [17592186298135 1111 #uuid "daf06b6c-0378-5ed2-8e42-a98b24450905" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298136 1034 "I only" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298136 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534601 true] [17592186298136 1111 #uuid "f04b233e-7483-5e00-bb08-e8bf3fbefe36" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298137 1034 "III only" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298137 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534601 true] [17592186298137 1111 #uuid "a13aa3bd-0364-5cf0-951f-83c0135be641" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298138 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298138 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534601 true] [17592186298138 1111 #uuid "2bc301fd-9324-5890-b98d-337dee9f4037" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298139 1034 "II only" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298139 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534601 true] [17592186298139 1111 #uuid "a198b3d6-9f9f-5e75-870c-bcef806c8cb6" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298140 1017 #edb/ref 17592186298141 13194139534601 true] [17592186298140 1089 "q-73499" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298140 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534601 true] [17592186298140 1100 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1), & x>1 \\\\ x-1, & x\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[0,2]$\n- $[-1,0]$\n- $[-1,2]$" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298140 1113 #uuid "78156bf9-5946-5f89-b672-be4ba96fe937" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298140 1145 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[0,2].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[-1,0].$ In this interval, $f(x)=x-1$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,0].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, in the interval $[-1,2]$, we also have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHence, $f(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,2]$ and therefore  the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139534601 true] [17592186298141 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298142 13194139534601 true] [17592186298141 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298143 13194139534601 true] [17592186298141 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298144 13194139534601 true] [17592186298141 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298145 13194139534601 true] [17592186298141 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298146 13194139534601 true] [17592186298141 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534601 true] [17592186298141 1041 "selection" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298141 1104 #edb/ref 17592186298146 13194139534601 true] [17592186298141 1110 #uuid "6ac6ef48-91db-5697-aaba-750f72ac0b5e" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298142 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534601 true] [17592186298142 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534601 true] [17